    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Part A §1, Part B Subpart A §§1 & 2, Subpart B §§1 & 2, Subpart C §1, Chap of 2021 (as proposed in S.471-A & A.3207)",
    "lawSection": "Real Property",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5742\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend a chapter of the laws of 2021 relating to establishing\nthe \"COVID-19 Emergency Protect Our Small Businesses Act of 2021\", in\nrelation to providing for certain commercial small businesses to be\neligible for relief under the \"COVID-19 Emergency Protect Our Small\nBusinesses Act of 2021\"\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow small businesses with up to 100 employees, or up to 500 employ-\nees if the business was shut down for a two-week period between May 15,\n2020 and May 1, 2021, to submit a hardship declaration under the COVID-\n19 Emergency Protect Our Small Businesses Act.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill allow small businesses with up to 100 employees, or up to 500\nemployees if the business was shut down for a two-week period between\nMay 15, 2020 and May 1, 2021, to submit a hardship declaration under the\nCOVID-19 Emergency Protect Our Small Businesses Act, and makes corre-\nsponding changes to the hardship declaration form in the Act.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nThe COVID-19 Emergency Protect Our Small Businesses Act of 2021 current-\nly allows businesses with up to 50 employees to submit a hardship decla-\nration.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill expands protections to more businesses that may be suffering\ndue to the economic impact of COVID-19.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nLOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nOn the same date and in the same manner as chapter 73 of the laws of\n2021.\n",
